WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.080
Hank: Good morning, John.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.580
So, by studying the traffic patterns of our
educational channel CrashCourse, I can tell

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.330
that students are currently freaking out and
watching 20 episodes of Crash Course Chemistry

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.799
back to back to back, and that can only mean
one thing: very soon, a large group of people

00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:15.000
will be graduating into the next stage of
their lives.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.690
And for those people, and also other people,
I have made this video.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.330
Hello, future dead person.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.529
I have an uncomfortable truth for you.

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:28.279
You are very likely one of the luckiest and
most powerful people who has ever existed

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:29.279
on this planet.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:34.010
Now, you almost certainly do not feel powerful,
it's difficult to feel powerful unless you

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.050
suddenly have more power than you once had
or have much more power than the people around

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.050
you.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.910
So you don't feel the power all the time,
and it's probably good that you don't, 'cause

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:47.110
it might be crippling, but in terms of absolute,
not relative, power, you are basically a god.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.810
You can hold the sum of all human knowledge
in the palm of your hand.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:55.500
If you've ever taken a hot shower on a cold
day, you have experienced a luxury that the

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.620
vast majority of humankind could never even
dream of.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:05.460
You can write down a thing or make a video
and potentially millions of people will be

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.540
affected by it.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.810
That's not normal.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.040
That's lots of power.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.960
So what's all that power for?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.460
Well, I'm not going to lie to you, this is
a complicated time.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.930
I, for example, have absolutely no practical
advice for you.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.170
Your future career is probably going to look
different than any other career that ever

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.940
happened ever, so, so you're gonna have to
be thoughtful and careful and maybe even a

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.920
little bit cunning, and by the time you reach
retirement age, jobs might not be a thing

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.370
anymore, so blehhh.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:34.270
There is one thing that I can definitely say
about the world as it exists right now.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.460
We are, at this moment, both creating and
solving problems faster than we ever have

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.460
before.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.270
So your job, the only thing anyone can ask
of you as a human, is to solve more problems

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.290
than you create.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.530
Also take care of yourself and have a good
time, ideally, but you are very powerful,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.210
and you can make your world and yourself better
so do that.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.399
As for how to do that, um, I've been thinking
a lot about Eve Online.

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:01.640
Eve is a massively multiplayer online space
capitalism game that takes place in the fictional

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.299
galaxy of New Eden and sprawls across more
than 7,800 different star systems.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:10.690
And just like in the real world, every player
in Eve decides for themselves what success

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:11.690
is.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.640
But overall, the culture of Eve is one of
theft and distrust and betrayal and greed.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.710
The developers of Eve didn't build this into
the world.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:23.360
That culture was defined by individual people
taking individual actions.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.690
Now this is interesting to me, because I also
live in a world that was created by the actions

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.580
of people--obviously, you know, the creation
of the world also had some stuff to do with

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:31.580
physics.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.180
But our culture is just the collective actions
and decisions of people.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.220
And that culture is, at this point, the single
most important factor in the health and sustainability

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.220
of our species.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.361
And we all get to collectively decide what
that culture is.

00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:45.420
Now, it might feel like we have no control
over this, that we're at the whims of what

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.370
has come before and that we're destined to
end up in a cutthroat world of ever-increasing

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:54.510
inequality, but not if we decide to not live
in that world, not if we choose compassion.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.850
We collectively decide what world we're gonna
live in by being that world.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.040
That's the real power you have.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.050
It's not your job, and it's not your bank
account.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.720
It's a power than every human has, every human
ever has had, and it has nothing to do with

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.770
immortality or fame and yet, I think that
it might be the most important thing that

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:09.780
every person does.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.970
And the reason we educate ourselves and improve
ourselves is so that we can be more effective

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:18.200
at making these positive changes, at creating
more solutions than problems.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.780
Because in the end, your actions are what
build our world, so don't take that for granted.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.090
And, John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

